                subtitle: "6 steps. No crystals. Just root cause analysis.",
                emoji: "🔍",
                sections: [
                    AwakeningSection(heading: "Step 1: Set Up Logging (Daily System Scan)", body: "You can't debug what you don't log.\n\nEvery evening, 5 minutes. Three questions:\n\n1. What emotion did I feel but not express today?\n   (This catches the memory leak in real-time)\n\n2. Where did I judge someone disproportionately?\n   (This catches projection — your shadow reflected in others)\n\n3. What pattern repeated?\n   (This catches the infinite loops)\n\nWrite it down. Not in your head — externally. In a notes app, a journal, a spreadsheet. Data you don't persist is data you lose.\n\nDo this for 7 days before touching anything else. You're gathering baseline metrics. Don't try to fix. Just observe and log.\n\nYou'll be shocked how quickly patterns emerge. Same emotion. Same trigger. Same response. Over and over."),
                    AwakeningSection(heading: "Step 2: Breakpoint — Catch the Bug Mid-Execution", body: "When you feel a disproportionate emotional reaction — tight chest, jaw clenching, sudden defensiveness, urge to check your phone, snap at someone, shut down — that's the shadow code executing.\n\nSet a breakpoint.\n\n1. PAUSE. Don't react. Don't suppress. Don't intellectualize. Just stop.\n\n2. NAME IT + LOCATE IT.\n   \"Anger. In my jaw and fists.\"\n   \"Shame. In my chest and stomach.\"\n   \"Fear. Throat tight, shoulders up.\"\n   Naming the emotion with its body location engages the prefrontal cortex, which starts down-regulating the amygdala. This is neuroscience, not philosophy.\n\n3. RATE IT. Intensity 1-10. This gives you quantifiable data over time.\n\n4. HOLD FOR 90 SECONDS.\n   Stay with the sensation. Don't add a story (\"They shouldn't have said that\"). Don't suppress it (\"I'm fine\"). Just observe the raw data — the physical sensation in your body.\n\n   Why 90 seconds? Neuroanatomist Jill Bolte Taylor's research: the chemical lifespan of an emotion in the body is approximately 90 seconds. If it lasts longer, you're re-triggering it with narrative. The feeling is brief. Your story about the feeling is what makes it chronic.\n\n   Most engineers have never sat with an emotion for 90 seconds without analyzing it. This is the hardest and most important skill in the entire protocol."),
                    AwakeningSection(heading: "Step 3: Stack Trace — Talk to the Bug", body: "This is Jung's \"Active Imagination\" technique. It sounds weird. It works. Think of it as rubber duck debugging for your psyche.\n\n1. Sit quietly for 10 minutes. Close your eyes.\n\n2. Think of the pattern — the repeating emotion, the trigger, the person who bothered you.\n\n3. Let an image appear. It might be a character, a younger version of you, a symbolic figure, whatever your unconscious serves up. Don't force it. Don't judge it.\n\n4. Talk to it. Out loud or in writing.\n   - \"What do you need?\"\n   - \"What are you protecting me from?\"\n   - \"When did you first show up?\"\n   - \"What happens if I let you go?\"\n\n5. Write down the dialogue.\n\nThis isn't meditation. It's not mysticism. It's accessing the stack trace — going below the surface error to find the root function call that started the cascade.\n\nMany engineers report that the \"character\" is a younger version of themselves — a scared kid, an angry teenager, a version of them from a specific memory. That version wrote the shadow code. It had good reasons at the time. The code was a survival mechanism. It just hasn't been updated since.\n\nYou're not deleting old code. You're having a conversation with the developer who wrote it, understanding their constraints, and then writing a better version."),
                    AwakeningSection(heading: "Steps 4-6: Refactor, Express, Observe", body: "STEP 4: WRITE THE PATCH\nVisualize the old pattern as actual code. See it. Then write the replacement:\n\nOLD: if vulnerable_moment { shutdown(); isolate(); work_harder(); }\nNEW: if vulnerable_moment { breathe(); name_feeling(); reach_out_to_trusted_person(); }\n\nRun the new code mentally 3 times. Visualize yourself in the trigger situation, executing the new behavior. This is cognitive rehearsal — used in CBT, military training, and elite athletics. Your brain can't fully distinguish vivid mental rehearsal from real experience. Same neural pathways fire.\n\nSTEP 5: EXTERNALIZE (Get it Out of RAM)\nShadow material that stays internal keeps executing as background processes. Get it OUT:\n\n• Draw the pattern as a system diagram or flowchart\n• Write the shadow's \"release notes\" — what it was trying to do, why it was built, what it cost\n• Move the energy through your body: walk fast, do pushups, box, shake, dance terribly in your room\n\nThe body stores what the mind suppresses. If you've ever felt inexplicably better after intense exercise, that's cached emotion being released from the physical system.\n\nSTEP 6: OBSERVER MODE (Meta-Cognition Upgrade)\nInstead of \"I AM angry\" → practice \"I NOTICE I'm experiencing anger.\"\n\nThis single shift is the difference between being inside the code and watching it execute from the IDE. You become the developer, not the runtime.\n\nResearch (Teasdale et al., MBCT studies): 8 weeks of this observer practice reduces rumination by 40-60%. Not mysticism. Controlled trials. Measurable outcomes.\n\nJung's most important line: \"Until you make the unconscious conscious, it will direct your life and you will call it fate.\"\n\nIn engineer terms: until you read your own source code, the legacy bugs will keep shipping to production and you'll keep blaming external factors."),
                ],
                reflectionPrompt: "Which of the 6 steps am I most resistant to? The logging? The 90-second hold? Talking to the pattern? That resistance IS the shadow protecting itself from review."
            ),
            
