            AwakeningLesson(
                id: "joa_m7b_l5",
                number: 5,
                title: "The 30-Day Sprint",
                subtitle: "Track it like any other engineering project",
                emoji: "📊",
                sections: [
                    AwakeningSection(heading: "Sprint Setup", body: "You wouldn't ship a feature without a timeline, metrics, and review cycles. Same here.\n\nDAILY TRACKER — 7 columns:\n• Date\n• Trigger (what happened externally)\n• Shadow Pattern Detected (which loop — personal, interpersonal, ancestral, cultural, archetypal)\n• Intensity (1-10)\n• Technique Used (which step from the protocol)\n• New Behavior Tested (what you did differently)\n• Outcome Metrics: Mood (1-10), Productivity (1-10), Sleep (1-10), Relationship Friction (1-10)\n\nWEEK 1-2: OBSERVATION SPRINT\nJust log. Don't fix. Gather baseline data. You will feel WORSE during this phase. This is normal. When you start logging suppressed emotions, they surface. It's like turning on the debugger for the first time — suddenly you see all the warnings you've been ignoring.\n\nThis is called a \"healing crisis\" in psychology. I call it \"debugging overload.\" The system is exposing bugs faster than you can fix them. It's temporary. Push through Week 2.\n\nWEEK 3-4: TARGETED REFACTORING\nBy now you'll see the same 2-3 patterns firing repeatedly. Those are your targets. Apply Steps 3-6 specifically to these patterns.\n\nEND OF SPRINT: RETROSPECTIVE\nCompare Week 4 metrics to Week 1. Most people report measurable improvement in at least 2 of 4 outcome metrics. If you tracked honestly, the data speaks for itself."),
                    AwakeningSection(heading: "What Engineers Report After 30 Days", body: "Based on clinical outcomes (Jungian analysis, IFS therapy) and anecdotal reports from technical professionals:\n\nFEWER REPEATING BUGS\nThe same argument with your partner doesn't happen the third time because you caught the projection in Week 2 and refactored the trigger response.\n\nBETTER SYSTEM STABILITY\nEmotional regulation under pressure improves. Not because you suppress more effectively — because there's less suppressed material causing random crashes. Your emotional runtime has fewer background processes hogging resources.\n\nHIGHER OUTPUT QUALITY\nCreativity increases when cognitive resources aren't being consumed by emotional suppression. Multiple fMRI studies confirm: suppressed emotions literally reduce prefrontal cortex function. Free up that RAM and your problem-solving gets sharper.\n\nCLEANER INTERFACES\nLess projection means you see people more clearly. Less defensiveness means better collaboration. Less need for validation means better leadership. Your API with other humans gets cleaner.\n\nTHE UNEXPECTED BENEFIT\nMany engineers report that shadow work made them BETTER engineers. Not because of any mystical reason — because understanding your own operating system makes you better at understanding ALL systems. Pattern recognition sharpens. Empathy for users improves. Tolerance for ambiguity increases. The skills transfer."),
                    AwakeningSection(heading: "When to Escalate", body: "Risk management. Read this carefully.\n\nESCALATE TO A PROFESSIONAL WHEN:\n• Material surfaces linked to trauma (abuse, violence, major loss)\n• You experience prolonged inability to function (can't work, can't sleep, can't eat)\n• Suicidal ideation appears — even briefly\n• Dissociation occurs (feeling detached from reality, watching yourself from outside)\n• The 30-day sprint makes things significantly worse with no improvement by Week 3\n\nThis is the equivalent of calling a senior engineer when you hit a production-critical bug you can't solve alone. Not weakness. Wisdom.\n\nLook for: Licensed psychologists trained in Jungian analysis, Internal Family Systems (IFS), EMDR, or somatic experiencing. These modalities specifically work with shadow/unconscious material. Avoid anyone who just gives you coping strategies without going deeper — that's putting a try/catch around the bug instead of fixing it.\n\nNEVER use shadow insights as weapons against others. Understanding someone's patterns doesn't give you permission to \"diagnose\" them or exploit their vulnerabilities. That's weaponizing the tools. Don't be that developer.\n\nTHE BOTTOM LINE\nYou don't have to believe in anything. Not God, not chakras, not the astral plane, not karma, not the Bible. None of that is required.\n\nAll you have to do is this: look at your own source code. Read the commits you've been ignoring. Talk to the parts of yourself you've been suppressing. Feel what you've been intellectualizing. And measure the results.\n\nJung spent decades collecting data. Modern neuroscience validates the mechanisms. IFS research confirms the model.\n\nShadow integration is root-cause analysis applied to your own mental operating system. Run the experiment. Track the metrics. Let the data speak.\n\nYour most important codebase isn't the one at work. It's the one running you."),
                ],
                reflectionPrompt: "Am I willing to commit to 30 days of honest self-observation? If not — what's the resistance? And what is that resistance protecting?"
